.TH CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(
    name,
    smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a ranged memory pool with the
given parameters, so that the caller can pass the buffer to
ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The size that the macro
computes includes the status byte of every element of every sub-pool, and
the buffer holds all the sub-pools in contiguous memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                                     Variable name for the buffer
smallest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The buffer goes on the stack or into static storage, depending on context
This is useful on embedded systems, and whenever you do not want a heap
  allocation
The macro declares the buffer with the alignment that every entry the
  pool hands out is guaranteed to meet, which is 16 bytes. You can
  therefore pass the buffer directly to
  ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() without having to
  think about alignment, and every single sub-pool segment inside the
  buffer stays correctly aligned. The 16 is a fixed constant; see
  CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(3) for the reason
The macro sizes the buffer exactly as
  ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() requires for the same
  parameters
A _Static_assert rejects three cases at compile time: a set of
  parameters that would make the required buffer size overflow size_t,
  a number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two below
  largest_size_power_of_two - smallest_size_power_of_two, and a
  smallest_size_power_of_two below 4. The smallest element that a ranged
  pool serves is 16 bytes, so a buffer declared for anything smaller
  could only go to a constructor that refuses it. Without the assert,
  the macro would silently produce an array of the wrong size
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 4, 8, 10);
ccol_r_mempool *rmp = ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 4, 8, 10,
    ccol_fallback_disabled, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
